-- title:
--   Sections of invertible modules frame off the zero scheme
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and $M$ an object of `X.Modules` which is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules on $U$. Let $s : \mathbf{1} \to M$ be a morphism from the unit object of `X.Modules` to $M$, and let $V$ be an open subset of $X$. Write $\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}\,s$ for the ideal sheaf datum on $X$ obtained as the infimum of all ideal sheaf data $J$ such that, for every affine open $U$ of $X$, the ideal $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U \subseteq \Gamma(X,U)$ — the ideal generated by the range of `coeff s U` — is contained in $J$ evaluated at $U$. Assume $V$, as a subset of $X$, is disjoint from the support of $\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}\,s$. Let $\sigma \in \Gamma(M, \top)$ be the image under $s$ at the level of global sections of the element $1 \in \Gamma(X,\top)$ viewed as a section of the unit module. The conclusion is that $\sigma$ is a frame on $V$: for every open $W$ of $X$ with $W \le \top$ and $W \le V$, the map $\Gamma(X,W) \to \Gamma(M,W)$, $g \mapsto g \cdot \sigma|_W$, is bijective.
--
--   This is the standard fact that a section of an invertible module trivialises it on the complement of the zero scheme of that section, here in the form that the section is a frame (a free generator of the module of sections) over every open subset disjoint from the support of the zero-scheme ideal sheaf. It connects the description of sections as morphisms out of the unit object with the frame predicate used for sections viewed as elements, and is invoked in the construction of trivialising covers and in isomorphism criteria for morphisms of invertible modules in the work on polarisations and the relative Picard functor.
